1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:02,306
Saving for the Emperor:

2
00:00:02,386 --> 00:00:08,300
The Occupation's Post Office
Savings Drives, 1942 to 1945

3
00:00:08,300 --> 00:00:12,957
On the 16th of July 1945, a
month before Japan surrendered,

4
00:00:13,037 --> 00:00:15,286
the Syonan Shimbun carried an

5
00:00:15,366 --> 00:00:19,300
advertisement from the
Malai Banking Association.

6
00:00:19,300 --> 00:00:21,290
"Fourth time!" it announced,

7
00:00:21,370 --> 00:00:26,020
offering another round of its
"Fixed Deposit with Bonus" scheme,

8
00:00:26,100 --> 00:00:29,500
with a savings encouragement
prize of $50,000.

9
00:00:29,500 --> 00:00:34,782
The banks would take deposits
from the 1st of August to the 31st

10
00:00:34,862 --> 00:00:40,475
of August 1945, and the prize draw
was set for the 31st of October.

11
00:00:40,475 --> 00:00:44,901
On the same page the Postmaster-General
told readers that money

12
00:00:44,981 --> 00:00:49,200
in the Post Office Savings Bank
would be "absolutely safe".

13
00:00:49,200 --> 00:00:53,539
For almost three years the Japanese
military administration had

14
00:00:53,619 --> 00:00:57,116
pressed the people of
Singapore and Malaya to save,

15
00:00:57,196 --> 00:01:02,517
through post office drives, bank campaigns
and prize draws, and by the middle

16
00:01:02,597 --> 00:01:06,875
of 1945 more than half a million
people had savings accounts.

17
00:01:06,875 --> 00:01:10,020
None of the money they
deposited during the

18
00:01:10,100 --> 00:01:13,100
Occupation was ever recognised after it.

19
00:01:13,100 --> 00:01:17,584
Singapore had had a post
office savings bank since 1877,

20
00:01:17,664 --> 00:01:22,148
run by the colonial government
across the counter of the

21
00:01:22,228 --> 00:01:27,200
General Post Office, as described
in an earlier post on POSB.

22
00:01:27,200 --> 00:01:31,675
Before the war it was a bank of
the Straits Settlements only.

23
00:01:31,675 --> 00:01:34,917
An account opened in
Singapore could not be used

24
00:01:34,997 --> 00:01:38,378
in the Federated Malay
States, and the other Malay

25
00:01:38,458 --> 00:01:41,850
states had their own
arrangements or none at all.

26
00:01:41,850 --> 00:01:44,712
When Singapore fell in February 1942,

27
00:01:44,792 --> 00:01:50,675
depositors could no longer draw on
those pre-war accounts as they pleased.

28
00:01:50,675 --> 00:01:54,916
Later in 1942 the Japanese
postal authorities

29
00:01:54,996 --> 00:01:59,237
let them take out part
of their old balances,

30
00:01:59,317 --> 00:02:03,350
at first $30 and then $25 every half-year.

31
00:02:03,350 --> 00:02:07,745
From the 8th of December
1942, the first anniversary of

32
00:02:07,825 --> 00:02:12,464
the war in the Pacific, the
limits were raised to $100 for

33
00:02:12,544 --> 00:02:17,100
the first withdrawal and $50
every half-year after that.

34
00:02:17,100 --> 00:02:20,100
The announcement came from Seiji Hamada,

35
00:02:20,180 --> 00:02:23,800
the Postal Administrator
for Malai and Sumatra.

36
00:02:23,800 --> 00:02:27,356
Malai was the administration's
name for Malaya,

37
00:02:27,436 --> 00:02:30,375
and Singapore had been renamed Syonan.

38
00:02:30,375 --> 00:02:34,315
In September 1942 the
administration opened a

39
00:02:34,395 --> 00:02:38,325
savings bank of its own
at the post offices.

40
00:02:38,325 --> 00:02:41,658
It later called it the
Dai Nippon Government

41
00:02:41,738 --> 00:02:44,375
Post Office Savings Bank of Malai.

42
00:02:44,375 --> 00:02:48,411
It paid interest of three per
cent a year, which the Syonan

43
00:02:48,491 --> 00:02:52,317
Shimbun pointed out was the
same rate as before the war,

44
00:02:52,397 --> 00:02:57,270
and unlike the old bank it was run
as one bank for the whole peninsula,

45
00:02:57,350 --> 00:03:01,675
so that an account could be used
at any post office in Malaya.

46
00:03:01,675 --> 00:03:06,143
In December 1942 the paper's
editorial quoted the Controller of

47
00:03:06,223 --> 00:03:10,474
Posts in Syonan, who described
savings as "the easiest means

48
00:03:10,554 --> 00:03:14,950
for the people living in the
Southern Regions to co-operate in

49
00:03:15,030 --> 00:03:19,650
the construction of the Greater
East Asia Co-Prosperity Sphere".

50
00:03:19,650 --> 00:03:24,384
The editorial went on: "it is indeed
the duty of every good citizen

51
00:03:24,464 --> 00:03:29,350
to save as much as he or she can
afford to put aside out of income."

52
00:03:29,350 --> 00:03:31,600
The start was slow.

53
00:03:31,600 --> 00:03:36,497
The Syonan Shimbun reported
that deposits across Malaya

54
00:03:36,577 --> 00:03:42,287
reached $500,000 in March 1943,
and that they took nearly a year

55
00:03:42,367 --> 00:03:47,525
to pass the first $1 million,
on the 20th of August 1943.

56
00:03:47,525 --> 00:03:51,570
The administration was paying
its way with its own new notes,

57
00:03:51,650 --> 00:03:54,883
the currency that came to
be called banana money,

58
00:03:54,963 --> 00:03:57,600
and it printed more of them every year.

59
00:03:57,600 --> 00:04:00,825
Money that people spent pushed up prices.

60
00:04:00,825 --> 00:04:03,799
Money that they put in
a savings account was

61
00:04:03,879 --> 00:04:06,725
out of circulation, at least for a while.

62
00:04:06,725 --> 00:04:11,657
The same thinking lay behind the
government lottery, which had been

63
00:04:11,737 --> 00:04:16,675
announced in 1942 as a way of
"absorbing excess purchasing power".

64
00:04:16,675 --> 00:04:21,634
The argument was put plainly in
February 1945, in a talk on Radio

65
00:04:21,714 --> 00:04:26,363
Syonan by Lim Beng Quan, a member
of the Auxiliary Police who

66
00:04:26,443 --> 00:04:31,250
had been given a certificate of
merit for encouraging savings.

67
00:04:31,250 --> 00:04:35,386
According to the Syonan Shimbun's
report, he told listeners

68
00:04:35,466 --> 00:04:40,530
that "national savings keep down
inflation and help to stabilize prices,

69
00:04:40,610 --> 00:04:43,325
thus keeping down the cost of living".

70
00:04:43,325 --> 00:04:46,193
He also acknowledged
the obvious objection,

71
00:04:46,273 --> 00:04:50,239
that "the average citizen does
not earn enough and the high

72
00:04:50,319 --> 00:04:53,942
cost of living is mounting",
and suggested that people

73
00:04:54,022 --> 00:04:57,450
give up some cinema visits
and cigarettes instead.

74
00:04:57,450 --> 00:05:01,700
The first big push came in September 1943.

75
00:05:01,700 --> 00:05:05,653
To mark the first million,
the postal department of the

76
00:05:05,733 --> 00:05:09,687
military administration held
a Post Office Savings Bank

77
00:05:09,767 --> 00:05:13,800
Promotion Week across Malaya
from the 1st of September.

78
00:05:13,800 --> 00:05:17,395
It issued commemorative
stamps of 8 and 15 cents,

79
00:05:17,475 --> 00:05:22,570
used a special date stamp at the
main post offices, and announced the

80
00:05:22,650 --> 00:05:26,925
results of a competition for
savings posters and slogans.

81
00:05:26,925 --> 00:05:30,692
In Syonan a travelling post
office toured the island,

82
00:05:30,772 --> 00:05:33,088
selling stamps and taking savings

83
00:05:33,168 --> 00:05:36,725
deposits at business
centres and amusement parks.

84
00:05:36,725 --> 00:05:41,313
According to the department's
figures, deposits in September

85
00:05:41,393 --> 00:05:46,215
were about three times the monthly
average before the campaign,

86
00:05:46,295 --> 00:05:49,638
and the total rose from
$1.11 million on the

87
00:05:49,718 --> 00:05:54,075
3rd of September to $1.4
million on the 11th of October.

88
00:05:54,075 --> 00:05:57,275
The banks joined in at the same moment.

89
00:05:57,275 --> 00:06:02,102
From the 1st of September 1943
all 14 member banks of the Malai

90
00:06:02,182 --> 00:06:06,541
Banking Association began
taking savings deposits as part

91
00:06:06,621 --> 00:06:11,450
of what the paper called a
"Toa-wide 'Save for Victory' move".

92
00:06:11,450 --> 00:06:16,045
Ordinary savings accounts paid two
per cent, up from one and a half,

93
00:06:16,125 --> 00:06:20,250
and a new kind of fixed savings
deposit paid three per cent.

94
00:06:20,250 --> 00:06:25,300
The drives that followed were tied
to the administration's calendar.

95
00:06:25,300 --> 00:06:29,760
A second promotion week ran
from the 15th of February to the

96
00:06:29,840 --> 00:06:35,208
21st of February 1944, the second
anniversary of what the administration

97
00:06:35,288 --> 00:06:39,450
called the "Birth of New
Malai", the fall of Singapore.

98
00:06:39,450 --> 00:06:42,924
Its commemorative date
stamp, used on passbooks as

99
00:06:43,004 --> 00:06:46,266
well as letters, showed
"a Malai father and son

100
00:06:46,346 --> 00:06:49,900
on their way to the Syonan
Jinja to pay respects".

101
00:06:49,900 --> 00:06:53,006
Picture slides of a
farmer setting out early

102
00:06:53,086 --> 00:06:56,200
to work and save were
shown in the cinemas.

103
00:06:56,200 --> 00:07:00,667
When savings passed $5 million
on the 21st of April 1944,

104
00:07:00,747 --> 00:07:06,410
depositors who showed their passbooks
at the post office were given free

105
00:07:06,490 --> 00:07:11,675
tickets to cinema shows from the
28th of April to the 7th of May.

106
00:07:11,675 --> 00:07:15,043
The notice said the shows
were meant "to enable

107
00:07:15,123 --> 00:07:18,492
the depositors also to
celebrate Tencho Setsu",

108
00:07:18,572 --> 00:07:21,800
the Emperor's birthday
on the 29th of April.

109
00:07:21,800 --> 00:07:25,318
There was also a competition
to guess the date

110
00:07:25,398 --> 00:07:29,075
on which postal savings
would pass $10 million.

111
00:07:29,075 --> 00:07:32,825
The banks' own campaign used prize draws.

112
00:07:32,825 --> 00:07:36,773
Under the Malai Banking
Association's "Fixed Deposit

113
00:07:36,853 --> 00:07:40,336
with Bonus" scheme, which
ran from the 15th of

114
00:07:40,416 --> 00:07:44,442
February to the 15th of
March 1944, every $100 placed

115
00:07:44,522 --> 00:07:48,550
on fixed deposit for a year
earned a lottery number.

116
00:07:48,550 --> 00:07:54,561
Each block of $2 million in
deposits carried prizes of $1,000,

117
00:07:54,641 --> 00:07:58,590
$500, $100 and $50, and one number across

118
00:07:58,670 --> 00:08:03,975
the whole scheme would win
a special prize of $10,000.

119
00:08:03,975 --> 00:08:08,847
The association reported that
about $8.5 million had come in,

120
00:08:08,927 --> 00:08:14,287
more than twice what it had expected,
and the numbers were drawn in

121
00:08:14,367 --> 00:08:19,400
public at the Kyoei Gekijo, a
cinema, on the 15th of May 1944.

122
00:08:19,400 --> 00:08:24,234
A second round followed in
October 1944, and by July

123
00:08:24,314 --> 00:08:28,850
1945 the association was
advertising its fourth.

124
00:08:28,850 --> 00:08:31,159
As the war turned against Japan,

125
00:08:31,239 --> 00:08:34,450
the drives reached
further into workplaces.

126
00:08:34,450 --> 00:08:38,612
In Penang in October 1944, the
Controller of Posts told the

127
00:08:38,692 --> 00:08:43,645
managers of large firms, trade
associations and amusement and gambling

128
00:08:43,725 --> 00:08:48,031
parks that "with the war now
reaching a decisive stage" their

129
00:08:48,111 --> 00:08:52,425
help "should be increased
manifold to ensure final victory".

130
00:08:52,425 --> 00:08:55,820
Under a new group savings
system, the managers would

131
00:08:55,900 --> 00:08:59,695
collect savings from their
staff and stallholders and send

132
00:08:59,775 --> 00:09:03,250
the names and amounts to
the post office each month.

133
00:09:03,250 --> 00:09:07,558
In Syonan, the Lim Beng Quan
talk of 1945 urged every member

134
00:09:07,638 --> 00:09:12,018
of the Auxiliary Police to keep
a savings book "to set a good

135
00:09:12,098 --> 00:09:16,479
example to their neighbours and
householders", and told every

136
00:09:16,559 --> 00:09:20,800
depositor to pay something in
"at least once every month".

137
00:09:20,800 --> 00:09:23,582
By then the travelling post office came

138
00:09:23,662 --> 00:09:26,450
"to his very door to collect savings".

139
00:09:26,450 --> 00:09:29,225
On paper, the drives worked.

140
00:09:29,225 --> 00:09:32,914
The Syonan Shimbun reported that postal

141
00:09:32,994 --> 00:09:36,683
savings across Malaya passed $5 million

142
00:09:36,763 --> 00:09:40,725
in April 1944 and $10 million in October.

143
00:09:40,725 --> 00:09:45,215
At the end of 1944 they stood at

144
00:09:45,295 --> 00:09:50,150
$16.6 million in 404,271 accounts.

145
00:09:50,150 --> 00:09:54,895
At the end of June 1945
they stood at $30.2

146
00:09:54,975 --> 00:10:00,731
million in 506,950 accounts,
against a pre-war peak,

147
00:10:00,811 --> 00:10:05,075
the paper said, of 180,000 depositors.

148
00:10:05,075 --> 00:10:08,150
Singapore's share grew with the rest.

149
00:10:08,150 --> 00:10:13,728
In April 1944 it had 25,472 depositors

150
00:10:13,808 --> 00:10:17,748
with $953,537 between them,

151
00:10:17,828 --> 00:10:22,810
and by the end of June 1945 it had

152
00:10:22,890 --> 00:10:28,250
99,378 depositors with $6.5 million.

153
00:10:28,250 --> 00:10:32,800
The rising line measured paper
money put away, not wealth.

154
00:10:32,800 --> 00:10:36,845
The same administration that
ran the drives was issuing

155
00:10:36,925 --> 00:10:41,425
notes faster every year, and
the notes lost value as it did.

156
00:10:41,425 --> 00:10:46,610
As the banana money post describes,
an egg that had cost 3 cents

157
00:10:46,690 --> 00:10:51,625
before the war cost about $100
by the end of the Occupation.

158
00:10:51,625 --> 00:10:56,035
Dividing the paper's own
totals by the number of

159
00:10:56,115 --> 00:11:01,368
accounts gives an average of
about $41 per account at the

160
00:11:01,448 --> 00:11:06,125
end of 1944 and about $60
at the end of June 1945.

161
00:11:06,125 --> 00:11:08,131
Measured in what they could buy,

162
00:11:08,211 --> 00:11:10,950
both sums were far smaller than they look.

163
00:11:10,950 --> 00:11:16,466
On the 16th of June 1945 the number
of depositors passed 500,000,

164
00:11:16,546 --> 00:11:18,618
and the postal department

165
00:11:18,698 --> 00:11:23,175
announced a commemoration
week from the 1st of July.

166
00:11:23,175 --> 00:11:28,125
A savings encouragement week
followed in Syonan in mid-July.

167
00:11:28,125 --> 00:11:31,992
On the 15th of July the
Postmaster-General, Mr Suzuki,

168
00:11:32,072 --> 00:11:37,109
spoke at an entertainment for
depositors at the Dai Toa Gekijo theatre

169
00:11:37,189 --> 00:11:41,575
and assured them that "their
money will be absolutely safe".

170
00:11:41,575 --> 00:11:46,775
The authorities, he said, "have
taken steps to meet any emergency".

171
00:11:46,775 --> 00:11:50,719
The same page of the Syonan
Shimbun reported that the first

172
00:11:50,799 --> 00:11:55,017
batch of people leaving Syonan
under an evacuation scheme would

173
00:11:55,097 --> 00:11:58,973
depart that evening, "to seek
safety up-country during the

174
00:11:59,053 --> 00:12:03,350
emergency period which threatens
the inhabitants of this city".

175
00:12:03,350 --> 00:12:06,578
Suzuki urged those leaving
to use the savings bank,

176
00:12:06,658 --> 00:12:09,561
so that they could draw
their money where they

177
00:12:09,641 --> 00:12:12,625
were going and avoid
losing it on the journey.

178
00:12:12,625 --> 00:12:15,819
The banks' fourth bonus scheme opened

179
00:12:15,899 --> 00:12:19,350
for deposits on the 1st of August 1945.

180
00:12:19,350 --> 00:12:22,863
Japan announced its surrender
on the 15th of August,

181
00:12:22,943 --> 00:12:28,530
while deposits were still being taken, and
British forces landed back in Singapore

182
00:12:28,610 --> 00:12:32,825
on the 5th of September, eight
weeks before the draw was due.

183
00:12:32,825 --> 00:12:37,263
In September 1945 the British
Military Administration

184
00:12:37,343 --> 00:12:41,350
declared that banana
money would have no value.

185
00:12:41,350 --> 00:12:45,087
When the Postal Savings Bank
reopened for withdrawals

186
00:12:45,167 --> 00:12:48,831
in October 1945, the Straits
Times reported that the

187
00:12:48,911 --> 00:12:51,639
old pre-war accounts would be honoured,

188
00:12:51,719 --> 00:12:56,400
provided depositors could produce
their passbooks or other proof.

189
00:12:56,400 --> 00:13:00,980
But "no withdrawals or enquiries",
the Straits Times reported,

190
00:13:01,060 --> 00:13:04,287
"can be entertained
regarding Postal Savings

191
00:13:04,367 --> 00:13:08,425
Bank accounts started during
the Japanese occupation".

192
00:13:08,425 --> 00:13:11,225
The position did not change.

193
00:13:11,225 --> 00:13:15,878
In February 1947 savings bank
officials in Kuala Lumpur told the

194
00:13:15,958 --> 00:13:22,461
Straits Times that deposits made during the
Occupation were "still considered worthless",

195
00:13:22,541 --> 00:13:25,863
and that any account
opened in the "Dai Nippon

196
00:13:25,943 --> 00:13:30,750
Savings Bank of Malai" was "not
recognised as a liability today".

197
00:13:30,750 --> 00:13:33,752
They added that withdrawals
made from the old

198
00:13:33,832 --> 00:13:36,971
British accounts during
the Occupation had been

199
00:13:37,051 --> 00:13:40,475
treated as withdrawals
"for value", so they stood.

200
00:13:40,475 --> 00:13:44,300
That combination hit
some depositors twice.

201
00:13:44,300 --> 00:13:47,838
A letter to the Straits
Times in August 1947,

202
00:13:47,918 --> 00:13:52,903
from a reader in Sungei Siput,
Perak, described a saver who had

203
00:13:52,983 --> 00:13:56,923
$1,000 in the bank when
the war began, drew it out

204
00:13:57,003 --> 00:14:01,425
early in the Occupation, and
later paid $1,000 back in.

205
00:14:01,425 --> 00:14:04,534
After the war he found
himself with nothing,

206
00:14:04,614 --> 00:14:08,600
because his withdrawal counted
and his deposit did not.

207
00:14:08,600 --> 00:14:13,123
In August 1949 another reader, from
Penang, wrote under the heading

208
00:14:13,203 --> 00:14:17,657
"A Raw Deal For The Small Man"
that the small saver's "withdrawals

209
00:14:17,737 --> 00:14:21,779
during the occupation are treated
as withdrawals" while "his

210
00:14:21,859 --> 00:14:26,325
deposits during the corresponding
period are completely ignored".

211
00:14:26,325 --> 00:14:29,650
Part of that was eventually put right.

212
00:14:29,650 --> 00:14:35,064
In 1950 the Federation of Malaya passed
a law to revalue such withdrawals,

213
00:14:35,144 --> 00:14:39,222
and on the 16th of February
1951 Singapore's Legislative

214
00:14:39,302 --> 00:14:44,270
Council passed a bill of its own
"for the revaluation of withdrawals

215
00:14:44,350 --> 00:14:49,250
from the Post Office Savings Bank
during the Japanese occupation".

216
00:14:49,250 --> 00:14:53,002
From May 1951, depositors
who had drawn on their

217
00:14:53,082 --> 00:14:57,153
pre-war accounts between
the 1st of January 1943 and

218
00:14:57,233 --> 00:15:01,304
the 5th of September 1945
could send their passbooks

219
00:15:01,384 --> 00:15:05,375
to the Controller of the
Post Office Savings Bank.

220
00:15:05,375 --> 00:15:09,617
Their withdrawals were to be revalued
on the sliding scale used for

221
00:15:09,697 --> 00:15:13,616
other wartime debts, which
converted occupation dollars into a

222
00:15:13,696 --> 00:15:18,067
smaller number of Straits dollars
according to the date, so that each

223
00:15:18,147 --> 00:15:22,275
wartime withdrawal counted for
less against the pre-war balance.

224
00:15:22,275 --> 00:15:25,090
The money paid into the occupation's

225
00:15:25,170 --> 00:15:28,225
own savings bank was never recognised.

226
00:15:28,225 --> 00:15:30,800
The pre-war bank went on.

227
00:15:30,800 --> 00:15:36,223
In 1949 Singapore's Post Office
Savings Bank became a separate

228
00:15:36,303 --> 00:15:40,573
institution from Malaya's,
and it grew into POSB,

229
00:15:40,653 --> 00:15:46,600
which DBS Bank acquired in 1998
and still runs under the POSB name.

230
00:15:46,600 --> 00:15:49,390
Today the protection for an ordinary

231
00:15:49,470 --> 00:15:52,100
saver is written down in advance.

232
00:15:52,100 --> 00:15:55,850
Under the Deposit Insurance
Scheme, administered by the

233
00:15:55,930 --> 00:15:58,636
Singapore Deposit Insurance Corporation,

234
00:15:58,716 --> 00:16:03,650
Singapore-dollar deposits in standard
savings, current and fixed deposit

235
00:16:03,730 --> 00:16:07,620
accounts are insured up to
$100,000 per depositor at each

236
00:16:07,700 --> 00:16:11,381
member bank or finance
company, and all full banks and

237
00:16:11,461 --> 00:16:15,500
finance companies in Singapore
are required to be members.

238
00:16:15,500 --> 00:16:20,360
Why it matters today: the savings
drives of 1942 to 1945 are a small

239
00:16:20,440 --> 00:16:24,791
part of the Occupation's history,
but they show how a wartime

240
00:16:24,871 --> 00:16:30,529
government could use the habit of thrift
to draw money back out of circulation,

241
00:16:30,609 --> 00:16:34,750
and how little protection that
left the people who saved.

242
00:16:34,750 --> 00:16:38,195
The deposits rose every month
in the official figures,

243
00:16:38,275 --> 00:16:41,589
while the currency they
were counted in lost most of

244
00:16:41,669 --> 00:16:45,325
its value and the government
that held them disappeared.

245
00:16:45,325 --> 00:16:49,585
A savings account is only as
safe as the money it holds and

246
00:16:49,665 --> 00:16:53,336
the institution that stands
behind it, which is why

247
00:16:53,416 --> 00:16:58,050
depositors today are told in
advance exactly what is protected.
